{
 "id": "pastores-a-belen",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Pastores a Belén",
 "description": "A Spanish-language Christmas villancico calling the shepherds to Bethlehem, as a Catholic songbook printed it for New Mexico in 1916, for 2 to 4 singers, sung in Spanish: the verse, \"Allí, allí, allí, nos espera Jesús\", and the refrain, \"Llevemos, pues, turrones y miel\" (let us bring nougat and honey) to offer the Child Manuel (Emmanuel).",
 "language": "es",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "villancico",
  "christmas",
  "folk"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 60,
 "key": "G",
 "timeSignature": "6/8",
 "pickupBeats": 0.333333,
 "credits": {
  "words": "unsigned, as printed in Cánticos espirituales con música (Las Vegas, New Mexico, 1916)",
  "tune": "unsigned, as printed in Cánticos espirituales con música (Las Vegas, New Mexico, 1916)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: printed 1916 in Cánticos espirituales con música, \"recogidos por la Revista Católica\" (Las Vegas, New Mexico), the music to the Cánticos espirituales \"recogidos por el P. Ralliere\"; no author named; Father J. B. Ralliere died in 1915",
  "tune": "public domain: printed 1916 in Cánticos espirituales con música, \"recogidos por la Revista Católica\" (Las Vegas, New Mexico); no composer named; Father J. B. Ralliere, who collected the Cánticos and whom Ellis (1955) says composed some of the songs still used in Tomé's services, died in 1915",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella; the book’s unsigned keyboard part was used only to check the chords",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "source": "tune and words from Cánticos espirituales con música, recogidos por la \"Revista Católica\" (Las Vegas, New Mexico: Revista Católica, 1916, copyright 1916 by the Revista Católica; \"A. M. D. G.\" at the head of the title), No. 23, \"Pastores á Belén\", pp. 20 and 21, among the songs \"Para la Noche Buena\": keyboard with the words between the staves, in F major in 6/8, the verse sung on the top note of the right hand and the refrain (\"Coro\") on its own staff in two voices; no author, composer or arranger is named on the page or in the index. The book's note \"¡¡¡IMPORTANTE!!!\" (leaf 9) says the songs were \"recogidos por el P. Ralliere\" and are printed as the people of the region \"así los conoce, así los canta\", left \"intactos\" even where they break the rules of the art. Father J. B. Ralliere, French-born pastor of Tomé, New Mexico, from 1858 to 1911, died on 18 July 1915 (Florence Hawley Ellis, \"Tomé and Father J. B. R.\", New Mexico Historical Review 30, no. 3, July 1955, pp. 217 and 218, quoting a townswoman's memorandum book, archive.org/details/sim_new-mexico-historical-review_1955-07_30_3, leaves 46 and 47). Read from the Gaylord Music Library (Washington University in St. Louis) scan, archive.org/details/b19151652/page/n19 (leaf 19, both pages). Written here a whole tone higher, in G (the tune D4 to E5), with every pitch and rhythm of the tune as printed, except: breaths written in for beginners, \"luz.\" and the first refrain's \"nuel,\" shortened from a quarter to an eighth and the \"Lle-\" after each sung a sixteenth late, so each \"Llevemos\" starts on a breath, and the refrain's \"nuel.\" before the closing \"al Niño Manuel\" shortened from a dotted quarter to a quarter; and the last note, a dotted half, shortened by the eighth-note pickup. The keyboard part is unsigned, so the four voices are our own, the piano used only to check the chords. Words as printed, accents included (\"Pastores á Belén\", \"A ver á nuestro bien\", where modern Spanish writes \"a\"); \"Para\" is printed under one eighth note and is sung there, quickly, as printed. The one verse printed with the music, and the refrain; the book sends singers to its words-only editions (1908 and 1913) for further verses.",
  "checked": null
 },
